<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="2-carboxy-1,4-naphthoquinol biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-5837" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Ubiquitous Plant Pathways (UPP) that was generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most land plants. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; |FRAME: DIHYDROXYNAPHTHOATE| is a branch point metabolite leading to the biosynthesis of |FRAME: Menaquinones menaquinone| (vitamin K2, in bacteria), |FRAME: 2-METHYL-3-PHYTYL-14-NAPHTHOQUINONE| (vitamin K1 in plants), and many plant pigments, including some two-ring naphthoquinones (such as |FRAME: CPD-9608| and |FRAME: HYDROXYNAPHTHOQUINONE|) and the three-ring anthraquinones (such as |FRAME: ALIZARIN| and |FRAME: CPD-9759|). These pigments are mostly found in |FRAME: TAX-24966|, where at least some of them are phytoalexins involved in plant defense. Many of these pigments were used as dyes since the early human history. The benzenoid ring (or A ring) of 1,4-dihydroxy-2-naphthoate is derived from |FRAME: CHORISMATE|, derived via the shikimate pathway. The quinone ring (or B ring) is formed by addition of |FRAME: 2-KETOGLUTARATE|. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; The first step of the pathway, conversion of chorismate to isochorismate, is catalyzed by |FRAME: EC-5.4.4.2| (MenF). Addition of the succinic semialdehyde moiety of 2-oxoglutarate to isochorismate, catalyzed by |FRAME: EC-2.2.1.9| (MenD), results in the formation of the unstable compound |FRAME: CPD-9924 &quot;SEPHCHC&quot;|. A pyruvoyl group is subsequently eliminated by |FRAME: EC-4.2.99.20| (MenH), resulting in the prearomatic compound |FRAME: CPD-9923 &quot;SHCHC&quot;|. It has been shown that |FRAME: CPD-9924 &quot;SEPHCHC&quot;| also undergoes a spontaneous isomerization to |FRAME: CPD-26609 &quot;iso-SEPHCHC&quot;|, though it is still not known whether this isomer is also an acceptable substrate for MenH |CITS: [35901288]|. Dehydration of |FRAME: CPD-9924 &quot;SEPHCHC&quot;| by |FRAME: EC-4.2.1.113| (MenC) results in the benzenoid aromatic compound |FRAME: O-SUCCINYLBENZOATE|. In a reaction that requires ATP hydrolysis, |FRAME: EC-6.2.1.26| (MenE) generates |FRAME: CPD-6972|. A dehydration reaction catalyzed by |FRAME: EC-4.1.3.36| (MenB) results in the formation of |FRAME: CPD-9925| (DHNA-CoA). The final reaction, hydrolysis of DHNA-CoA to form |FRAME: DIHYDROXYNAPHTHOATE|, is catalyzed by |FRAME: EC-3.1.2.28| (MenI) |CITS: [23564174]|.</Description>
